<Global title="About Us" gutter="0.5em">
	<h1>About Us</h1>

	<h2>What’s in a name?</h2>
	<p>
		Nearly everyone asks the same few questions: What does <em>eunoia</em> mean?
		and How do I pronounce it?
	</p>
	<p>
		Feel free to invent your own pronunciation, as there is no definite answer.
		Personally we suggest ( <em>u . noy . ah</em> ). Eunoia. Simple!
	</p>
	<p>
		<em>Eunoia</em> as a word derives from the Greek <em>εύνοια</em> meaning “Beautiful
		thought” or “Well mind”. In English it is a little known word that has come to
		be used in medical circles as meaning “normal mental health” ‒ literally “well
		mind”. We prefer the “Beautiful thought” translation, hence the slogan.
	</p>
	<p>
		As a word though it’s claim to fame doesn’t end there; it’s first written
		example is in the works of Aristotle, no less, to describe the mindset that
		leads to harmony.
	</p>
	<p>
		It is also the shortest word in the English language to contain all five
		vowels. Plus “n”, obviously.
	</p>
	<p>
		So why did we choose it for a web design company? To begin with we fell for
		the word itself. It’s wonderfully clean, simple and neat ‒ six letters, five
		vowels and it rolls off the tongue. These factors alone piqued our interest;
		we pride ourselves on producing clean, simple and neat designs and
		interfaces after all.
	</p>
	<p>
		Then there was the appeal of weirdness, a little bit of mystique and a whole
		lot of individuality that appealed to our nature both professionally and on
		a personal level. Eunoia fit perfectly and really embodies our business:
		unique, concise and a little out-of-the-box.
	</p>

	<h2>Confusion is a lack of Eunoia</h2>
	<p>
		Another question we receive frequently upon explaining the logic behind the
		name is “Do we think it’s memorable? Isn’t it a little complicated?”
	</p>
	<p>
		Of course we had to muse this over ourselves; we tried countless names and
		brands before settling on Eunoia. Is it memorable? Yes. Is it complicated?
		Yes. But that’s not always a bad thing. For example in 1996 a small company
		began setting down roots under a name no-one understood. Today they sit atop
		a global empire, rivalling Apple and Microsoft. Their name? Google.
	</p>
	<p>
		Of course to have ambitions that high would be highly pretentious but the
		hope is there. If terms such as IRC, Yahoo, Mibbit, Twitter and Facebook can
		enter the mainstream, hopefully so too can Eunoia.
	</p>

	<h2>Who Is We?</h2>
	<p>
		Often on the site and the blog we write about “we” (just read that sentence
		for an example) and so occasionally are asked “who” we are. Eunoia is a
		small, start-up company but there are two of us at the helm. Murray and
		Mike; splitting the job down the centre. Murray takes care of the coding,
		the unseen force behind a website. Mike adds the gloss by applying design to
		maximise aesthetics and functionality. Everything done in Eunoia’s name is
		done by us both, hence the “we”.
	</p>

	<h2>Segregated Thoughts</h2>
	<p>
		Some people get confused with the “Thoughts” bars in the side panel of the
		blog. Why? Because unlike most places we have two: one for us and for
		everyone else. The logic behind this at first seems lacking <em>however</em>
		it makes more sense if you know that Our Thoughts is actually a Twitter feed.
		What’s Twitter? That’s an unrelated questions, you can go here to find out more.
	</p>
	<p>
		The benefits of a Twitter feed? Other than recording when we “tweeted” there
		really is only one benefit, but it is a large one. We can “tweet” (post a
		thought) from anywhere via text message. This means that we can update the
		blog at a micro-level on the fly, from anywhere, and allows us to stay in
		contact with readers. It’s a little bonus that we feel makes the blog a
		whole lot more personal, which is always a good thing.
	</p>
</Global>

